{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792406123,"nonce":"4wGS2Uen/iuxcm9RmElq+3JeOfI=","title":"Invalid code compiles when the fallback parser drops what it cannot read","message":"When the recursive-descent parser rejects a file, `parse_haxe_file_with_config` (parser/src/haxe_parser.rs) prints a warning to stderr (\"RD parser failed ... falling back to legacy parser\") and retries with the nom parser. That parser can succeed on the same text by silently dropping the part it cannot read. The compile then reports no error and runs a different program.\n\nRepro: in a class with several methods, replace a statement in the last method with `trace(p.mo` (unclosed call, no `;`). The LSP received no diagnostic, and the typed file lacked the method body. A minimal one-method file does fail in both parsers (`Main.hx:5: characters 5-6 : Expected , or )` from haxe).\n\nThe RD failure is a real syntax error in these cases and should be the reported error. The LSP now runs the RD parser itself before compiling and publishes its errors, but every other entry point still accepts the dropped-code result. Related: ae08eb3 (missing `;` accepted).","files":null}]}